  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 158 PM EDT Mon Oct 26 2026\n\nFairly quiet pattern continues across the area. Main concern\nfor this shift has been the potential for fog overnight tonight\ninto tomorrow morning. A west to east weak stationary boundary \nfeature currently is in place over northern IN. Right now the\ncurrent thinking is that the southwestern portions of the CWA\nhave the better chance at fog as winds further north and east\nlook to be a bit stronger keeping the lower levels better mixed \nand amount of moisture will also be lower in those locations\nwhich will help to keep fog development at bay. The other\nquestion is how much mid level clouds associated along the\nboundary develops which would also hinder radiational cooling.  \nThat is why there is a bit of uncertainty with this forecast in\nparticular as any wavering of the boundary would change things.\nSo will be keeping a close eye on things tonight. \n\nOtherwise, dry conditions will primarily continue with a fairly zonal\nflow aloft in place through the remainder of the week. However, \non Thursday a weak disturbance ripples through just to the north\nof the area and may bring light rain showers and even a rumble\nof thunder for areas mainly north of US-6 and especially for\nareas near and adjacent to Lake Michigan. Temperatures through\nthe week look to remain above normal with highs in the 70s and\nlows generally in the upper 40s to mid 50s. Next week the longer\nrange guidance does show the pattern in place now beginning to\nbreak down and becoming potentially more active. The 6-10 day\noutlook does show above average chances for precipitation and\nmuch above normal temperatures.",
